00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:21.782
of how (indistinct) unscathed and managed

00:01:21.782 --> 00:01:23.009
to come out the other side

00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:24.753
and finally, it feels kinda normal

00:01:24.753 --> 00:01:27.741
that we're having science
on Saturdays once again.

00:01:27.741 --> 00:01:29.931
So join me in welcoming Albert.

00:01:29.931 --> 00:01:31.710
(audience applauding)

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:32.910
- Well, thank you very much Kurt

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:36.720
for the really very nice introduction

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.010
and I really feel privileged
that I'll be the first speaker

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.753
after the long pandemic.

00:01:44.193 --> 00:01:45.150
And it's certainly important

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.620
for all the kids who have
missed out on science on Sunday

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.200
during the last two or three years.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.610
So thank you very much for
the kind invitation to speak.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.020
And I'm gonna talk, of course,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.760
about everyone's favorite subject now

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.410
that we've left the pandemic.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.893
But just before that, how
many here are scientists?

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.160
All right, the kids, how many
want to be scientists? Yeah?

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.060
Okay.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.150
All right, let's get into it.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:20.150
So the topic of today's
lecture is how we use science

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.480
to protect people from epidemics.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.770
Kind of an important subject
now that we've come out

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.630
of a large pandemic.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.210
And, you know, and so
I'll go through today

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.670
about what are kind of
maybe the nuts and bolts

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:39.723
in how we approach these
epidemics or pandemics.

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:45.510
It doesn't turn out to be like this.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.320
You won't unfortunately see
all these cast of actors.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.011
Everybody seen this movie?

00:02:51.011 --> 00:02:51.844
- [Audience Member] No.

00:02:51.844 --> 00:02:52.740
- [Albert] No? All right.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.580
Okay. Well, you probably
know some of the actors.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:59.430
Okay so, you know, and
it's actually a good movie.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:00.600
I liked it.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.170
It actually tells you some
of the nuts and bolts.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.230
You won't be meeting these
people on any of your outbreak

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.743
or epidemic or pandemic investigations,

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:16.610
but I would refer you to seeing the movie.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.620
One thing is, is that, you know,

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:24.620
today's subject is really
on science and what we try

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.870
to react to or try to address
is, nothing spreads like fear

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.840
and the answer to fear is science, okay?

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.560
So what I'll go through today
is how I became interested

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.393
in science.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.340
I'm an epidemiologist as well

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.500
as an infectious disease physician.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.280
And the young kids,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.833
how many know what an epidemiologist does?

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:51.660
All right, good.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:52.890
It's kind of a funny word,

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:56.430
so I'll go through what an
epidemiologist does in practice

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.123
and how we use science to
prevent infectious diseases.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.210
I'll go through what causes kind

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:04.950
of the new infectious threats

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:09.510
that have emerged and are going to emerge.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:12.150
And then how do we use science to respond

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.443
to those new threats?

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:17.040
And I'll just give some
advice at the end, you know,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.523
for young budding
scientists in the audience.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.390
So let me start, and let me
start a story, not my story

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:27.870
but my story of my parents
and their influence.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:32.870
My mother was a mathematician
and she came to America

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.593
to study, her freshman year in Boston,

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.980
and there she met my father.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.290
My mother was the person who
actually gave me the love

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.386
of science, you know, through mathematics,

00:04:45.386 --> 00:04:48.420
through chemistry, putting together

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.290
as a high school student,
molecules and so forth.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.210
My father was an engineer.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.420
He didn't really have as much
of an interest in science,

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.440
but what he did have

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:02.700
that motivated me was a
disease called tuberculosis.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.473
My parents met in Boston.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.270
My father came, I think in 1953

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.730
and very typical immigrant story,

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:15.630
but within two or three months
started coughing up blood.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:17.790
He was at MIT at that time.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.470
They immediately took him,

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.110
put him in what we call a sanitarium.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.630
This was a refuge for where people

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:28.230
with tuberculosis got
treated and he stayed there

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.540
for two years.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.330
This is kinda the typical
chest x-ray associated

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:34.170
with tuberculosis.

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:36.690
You can kinda tell that
this side is more wider

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:37.800
than that side.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.828
This side actually has a
cavity that's filled chock full

00:05:41.828 --> 00:05:44.700
of the bacteria that causes tuberculosis.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.670
And in those days, you put
people into the hospitals

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.890
and these sanitariums
and they stayed there.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:53.910
My father got daily
injections of new antibiotics

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:58.350
that came out to save the lives
of people with tuberculosis.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:01.530
Not only my father had
tuberculosis, but my whole family,

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:06.530
my grandmother, my aunts
in Korea had tuberculosis.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.990
And that really highlights,

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:14.280
what I think is a really unique side

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.990
of what we do as scientists,

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:18.840
and that's our contributions to society.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:23.840
And these are 10 of the greatest
public health achievements

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.790
that happened not in your lifetime,

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:28.170
but your grandparents' lifetime

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.720
and great grandparents' lifetime.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.330
Two of these are really very important

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.250
for infectious diseases.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.860
Infectious diseases was the leading killer

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.705
of Americans as well as
most people in the world

00:06:41.705 --> 00:06:43.770
in the last century.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:47.160
And these great public health
achievements, all of these,

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.340
including ones like family
planning or fluoridation

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.340
of drinking water for dental care,

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.543
all of these were driven by science.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.620
And just to give you an idea of what you,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:06.620
as scientists, can do to
contribute to society,

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:09.200
it's really captured in this slide.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.463
It is just a very simplified slide.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:17.280
Eight hundred thousand
Americans in 1900 died

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.863
of an infectious disease each year.

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:25.320
You can see that this sharp
decline in the deaths due

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:29.400
to infectious diseases were
really done to great advances.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.010
Some of them required vaccines,

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.440
some of them required antibiotics.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.800
Many of these things required
what we in public health

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.090
and epidemiologists do:

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:44.090
clean water, good housing,
secure housing and social rights,

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:49.613
rights to wellbeing,
which created this great,

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.340
large decline in deaths.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.430
You can see there's one
spike up here, right?

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.400
And we've just underwent a spike like that

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.500
with the COVID pandemic.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:06.090
This was the great
influenza pandemic of 1918

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:10.680
which killed hundreds of
millions of people worldwide

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.150
at that time.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:17.150
But much of these advances
in public health were all due

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.970
to science with different teams of people,

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:23.850
different disciplines within
science working together

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:27.003
to make this large societal benefit.

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:30.930
And that benefit didn't only happen here

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:34.530
in the United States, but
happened throughout the world.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.360
This shows, this is my lifetime.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:40.620
I was born around this time
and within my lifetime,

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.820
we've seen dramatic
decreases in little babies,

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:49.050
in little infants dying of
diseases, mostly diarrhea,

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:54.050
malaria, pneumonia, meningitis.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:58.800
This time, even in the
world's poorest countries,

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.570
we've seen sharp declines.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.660
And with that decline in infants dying,

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.800
we've had large increases
in life expectancy not only

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.360
in the richest countries, but
also in the poorest countries.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.360
Much of this was driven
by infectious diseases,

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.360
our advances in infectious
diseases, sanitation, hygiene,

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.890
social rights such as good
housing, as well as vaccination.

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:27.660
Many of you who had diarrhea,

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:31.380
you probably maybe took
Pedialyte, this kind of salt,

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:36.380
sugar formula to prevent
the long-term consequences

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:41.370
of diarrhea, on malnutrition.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:43.833
And so that was kind of my calling.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:49.820
As Kurt said, I was a chemist
when I was an undergraduate

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.207
and one of my professors said that,

00:09:52.207 --> 00:09:53.340
"Why don't you do something

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:55.230
that maybe could have potentially,

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.360
a broader impact socially?

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.120
Why don't you go back to what
your father's story was?"

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.850
This was one of my first
mentors, Evan Scrimshaw,

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:07.230
who was one of the champions
of nutrition, good nutrition,

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:11.850
sound nutrition for all back
in the 1960s, 70s and 80s.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:15.003
And that took me, after I
finished my medical training,

00:10:15.901 --> 00:10:17.100
I went to medical school,

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:20.917
did my residency in internal
medicine and then said,

00:10:20.917 --> 00:10:22.920
"Okay, where should I go?"

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:26.430
And the place that my mentors
at that time told me to go

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:28.170
to was to go to Brazil

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:30.903
and I actually went to
a city called Salvador.

00:10:32.266 --> 00:10:35.520
I hope all of you know in
Brazil, it's in South America,

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:37.110
then America is up here.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:39.420
This city is in the
Northeast of the country.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:42.270
It's the oldest city in Brazil.

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:47.270
90% of the population is
African Brazilian descent

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:50.370
because of the intense slave trade.

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:53.520
It's the poorest area
of the country as well.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.980
And this city, like many others in Brazil

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:57.180
but throughout the world,

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:59.910
especially the poorest
countries, look like this.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:03.150
You can see these high
rises where the rich live

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:07.230
and you can see these
disorganized areas here.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:10.290
They call these in Portuguese,
(speaks in Portuguese),

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:14.400
islands of inequality that
occur within these cities.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.080
And this type of phenomenon,

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:20.250
the have and the have-nots
exist throughout the world.

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:22.110
And to kinda show you,

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:26.460
this is taking a public health
perspective why that happens,

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.400
so the first time in human history,

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:34.260
we have more people in
2007 living in cities

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.270
than we did in the countryside.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:38.943
This is all due to the
massive growth in cities,

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:42.390
the poor people moving
from the countryside

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:45.030
to the cities.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:49.470
And by 2037, and this is when
you guys will all grow up,

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:53.580
the majority of the world's
population will live in cities

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:58.168
in the poorest areas, poorest
countries of the world.

00:11:58.168 --> 00:11:59.180
Okay.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:04.180
And that change in
urbanization happens together

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.740
with changes in what we
call social inequality,

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:10.800
the have and the have-nots,
the rich and the poor.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:11.910
And you can see this

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:14.850
and one of the reasons
that my mentors told me

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:18.390
to go to Brazil is exactly
captured in this picture.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:20.730
You can see very clearly that one wall

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:24.420
that separates the have
and the have-nots, okay?

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.040
You see on this wall,

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:28.830
you can see everybody has an
apartment with a swimming pool.

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:30.720
They have their tennis courts.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:34.800
These people go more to
Disney World in Florida

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.360
than they ever will jump over

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:39.870
on this other side of the fence,

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:43.110
and that side of the fence
is what we call urban slums

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:46.500
or urban, informal settlements.

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.750
Together in the world's population,

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:50.580
the roughly seven, eight billion people,

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:53.760
one billion of the world's population live

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:56.040
in these type of conditions.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:58.500
In these conditions of
disorganized growth,

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:00.300
people are squatters.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:03.600
They don't own the land that they live on,

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:06.120
and this picture tells that story.

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.880
This picture is from
where we've been working

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.820
for the last 25 years in Brazil.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:16.820
But this picture, you can
take in Lagos, in Africa,

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:20.190
you could take that picture
in Manila, in the Philippines.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:23.070
You can see this picture in
many places of the world.

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:27.270
You can see this is a community
where half the people live

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:29.613
on less than $2.50 per day.

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.120
This is a community that's densely packed,

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:36.900
so people are huddled together,
many times, four or five,

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:40.080
six people in a small, small house.

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:43.560
So you can imagine transmission
of diseases occurring

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:45.780
in these very crowded settings.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:48.330
You can see that there's
a lack of sanitation,

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:52.320
these open sewers and
there's no refuse collection,

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:54.600
taking out things that
we take for granted here

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:55.653
in New Haven.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:59.733
And in this environment,

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:04.413
you know, which has happened
due to ecological changes,

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:08.130
50 years ago if you took this picture,

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:11.310
it would be a pristine
Atlantic rain forest.

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:15.600
Now it's a densely crowded
urban, informal settlement.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.210
And here in this type of environment

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:21.090
over what happened over my lifetime,

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:25.050
the most successful
mammalian species, okay,

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:28.470
the most successful mammalian animal here,

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:30.420
is not human beings,

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:33.990
but it's related to all these
little holes that you can see

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:35.643
in this earth and stairwell.

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.238
Does anybody know what
those holes are? Yeah.

00:14:39.238 --> 00:14:41.130
- [Child] Of like, mouses or rats.

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:42.180
- [Albert] Rats, yeah.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:43.260
That's where the burrows,

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:46.500
the nests where the
rats make their burrows.

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:50.370
So this is, you can see, very
close to where people live,

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:53.103
all these burrows where rats are.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.650
And this actually leads to
what we call a plague of rats.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.320
This is an article in the science magazine

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:04.920
about the projects we've been
doing in the communities.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.200
You can set out traps here
and capture dozens of rats

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:11.610
in a night.

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:14.430
And there are certain
diseases that we worry about,

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:16.770
and we'll be talking
about this a couple times

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:19.080
'cause this is important for pandemics,

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:22.440
but there are diseases
that are carried by animals

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:26.370
that can spill over and spread to humans,

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.110
and in fact cause disease.

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:31.020
One of these is a disease that I never saw

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:32.910
when I was here in the United States,

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:37.530
but I saw a lot the moment I
got off the airplane in Brazil,

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:39.960
is a disease called leptospirosis.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.520
It's caused by a bacteria,

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:44.850
a very thin kinda corkscrew like bacteria.

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:48.273
That corkscrew is important
because that corkscrew,

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.260
sorry, the video's not working

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:54.510
but that corkscrew allows the bacteria

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.430
to travel very quickly,

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:58.533
kind of like a screw going through water.

00:16:00.611 --> 00:16:04.050
And that's important because
these bacteria hang out

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:06.960
in the kidneys of these animals,

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:08.220
many different types of animals,

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:13.220
but one important animal
that carries it is rats

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:17.160
and they shed it out as they
urinate into the environment.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:22.160
They shed loads of these
bacteria into the environment.

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:24.900
And these bacteria can swim

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:26.730
and when they encounter a human,

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:30.993
they can bore through
the skin or, you know,

00:16:32.086 --> 00:16:35.130
let's say the tissues in
your mouth and your eyes.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:38.430
They can bore through
and then they can spread,

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:42.870
proliferate and cause a massive infection.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:45.060
And those people who get infected,

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:47.400
they come down with renal failure,

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:50.640
they lose their ability
to their kidney function.

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:55.020
They also come out with massive
bleeding from the lungs,

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:57.843
you know, what we call hemorrhagic fever.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.660
And several days after
I got off the plane,

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:07.380
hospitals were filled with these cases.

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:08.280
And what we saw

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:10.680
when we did our first
outbreak investigation,

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:13.230
sort of like that team in that
first slide for "Contagion,"

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:14.910
we got all these people together,

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:18.570
we started to do an
outbreak investigation.

00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:20.520
Back in 1996,

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:24.360
the hospital was filled with
patients with this disease.

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:25.650
Our intensive care unit

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:29.340
where we treat the sickest
people were all getting,

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:33.180
you know, dialysis because
of the kidney failure.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:37.260
And we had to put them up to
hook them up to a ventilator

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:41.307
because they were bleeding
profusely outta their lungs.

00:17:41.307 --> 00:17:44.880
And you can see that every time it rains,

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:48.660
you have these large
epidemics of this disease.

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:52.650
Now I'll talk a little bit
later about why that happens,

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:55.290
but these are outbreaks or epidemics

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:59.160
that happen in these
vulnerable populations,

00:17:59.160 --> 00:18:02.460
living in those poor,
urban slums every year,

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:06.540
very predictable outbreaks
that occur same time,

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:09.933
same communities, same people.

00:18:11.579 --> 00:18:12.720
So what do we do about that?

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:15.963
And that's why we have epidemiologists,

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:19.830
who are scientists
dedicated for the purpose

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:22.710
of public health to keep people safe.

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:26.190
So epidemiology, and I
think through these talks,

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:28.690
you'll learn a lot about
all the different ologies

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:31.320
and all the different types of sciences,

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:36.320
but epidemiology is the study
of the distribution and causes

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:39.060
of diseases or health related states

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:41.430
in specified populations.

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:46.290
And I think most
importantly, it's the science

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:50.610
of how we control those
health problems that arise.

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:54.930
That's kind of our job to do,
and it's actually a fun job.

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:59.070
You know, many times we're
called into very bad situations.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.230
The COVID pandemic was one.

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:05.250
But many times, the fun part of this is

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:07.530
that we're always doing something new,

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:09.330
always learning something new,

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:11.760
but we're also working with a
lot of different scientists.

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:14.850
We're working with chemists like Kurt.

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:18.120
We're working with
sociologists, economists,

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:22.050
microbiologists, people who
study bacteria and viruses.

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:24.450
We work with a lot of different people,

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:27.960
so we learn a lot and have fun
doing that as well as trying

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:32.960
to do something good like
controlling health problems.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:36.210
And you can see that many times

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:39.450
if you're an infectious
disease physician like I am,

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:44.040
many times we just think about
the patient and the pathogen

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:46.650
or the virus or the bacteria.

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:48.360
But if you're an epidemiologist,

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.613
you have to think about
all of these things.

00:19:51.961 --> 00:19:52.830
If you go back to the slides,

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:55.680
you have to think about
how's the environment,

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.300
how much people are living on.

00:19:57.300 --> 00:19:58.470
Are they able to survive?

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.950
Are they being able to
be nourished properly?

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:05.610
Do they have some genetic
factors or biological factors

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:08.850
that may influence them
getting sicker than others?

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:11.400
And also, are there other structures

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:14.820
or other factors like social factors,

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:17.520
the type of leader that you have

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:21.540
that influences how well
people are gonna get treated

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:24.750
or how well we're gonna
prevent the disease?

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:27.630
So this encompasses all of epidemiology,

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:30.543
and that's what makes everything fun.

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:35.137
And the second thing that
we did was not only what

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:39.663
to study, but how we're gonna study it.

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:45.390
So this is a program
that we started a year

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:47.730
after I stepped off the plane.

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:50.947
We already done several outbreak
investigations and we said,

00:20:50.947 --> 00:20:53.370
"Hey, you know,

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:56.490
what we need to do is to
train more scientists,"

00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:59.850
scientists like you in the audience

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:03.600
and we had to train them
in many different things.

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:07.680
So we started this back in 1996.

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:10.380
One of the most important parts is

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:15.360
that the best ways to prevent disease is

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:17.460
to do this through the community,

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:21.000
not sitting in a university or not sitting

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:22.920
in a research institute,

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:25.590
but actually working in the community.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:29.610
And so one of our foundational
principles was community

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:31.230
as equal partners.

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:33.210
And what we did is similar to what Kurt

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:38.210
and everyone is doing here,
is that we had high school,

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:43.530
headstart programs,
college work study programs

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:47.290
in the communities that were
affected by these diseases

00:21:49.752 --> 00:21:51.720
so we could train scientists

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:54.540
who can solve their own
problems, where they live,

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:57.567
rather than have somebody outside come in.

00:21:57.567 --> 00:22:00.393
And so these are actually
our high school students.

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:02.610
You can see that, you know,

00:22:02.610 --> 00:22:04.500
we teach 'em how to use a computer

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:06.840
but one of the key things in the computer

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:09.660
that you can do is you can analyze data.

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:12.570
You can analyze large amounts of data,

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:15.600
and we can get that
data in kinda cool ways.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:17.880
One of the things that we
do is give the students,

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:21.300
we have them go out and
we give them GPS devices,

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:24.360
almost like Google does
when they go by your house.

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:25.560
We give them these cameras

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:27.900
and they can just take
pictures along the way.

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:29.760
We also give them drones.

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:32.277
The kids like that, you
know, kids like that a lot.

00:22:32.277 --> 00:22:35.580
And so these kids are
going out, using drones

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:37.320
to find out all the places

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:41.059
that may be potentially
transmitting the diseases

00:22:41.059 --> 00:22:42.210
that we're working on.

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:46.410
Here, you can see some students
here that are going around

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:48.600
and surveying all the places

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:50.850
where diseases like leptospirosis,

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:53.190
that rat borne disease, could occur.

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:57.720
And we use this to address
a whole bunch of problems.

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:00.600
One of the big killers when
I came down was meningitis,

00:23:00.600 --> 00:23:03.450
a disease that we hardly see
here in the United States,

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:06.753
but a disease that we could
prevent with vaccines,

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:11.043
or diseases that are
transmitted by mosquitoes.

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:16.650
But let's go back to leptospirosis.

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:20.040
So this is a disease where
you have to have the rat

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:25.040
because the rat's the animal
which kinda sheds the bacteria

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:27.810
that infects people.

00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:32.490
And this is a very typical scene.

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:36.640
This is the backyard of
almost everybody who lives

00:23:39.337 --> 00:23:41.010
in the study site where
we've been working,

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:43.020
where I showed those photos

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:46.803
and you can see that they're
chock full of rats, okay?

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:51.630
Rats, I don't know if anyone
here has captured rats.

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:54.090
I did it for about five years and gave up

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:59.090
and let the students do it,
but not easy to capture rats.

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:01.140
You have to go out in
the middle of the night.

00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:02.793
Sometimes it's not safe.

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:07.770
Sometimes they don't like
the bait you give them.

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:10.500
We give them Gorgonzola cheese and bacon,

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:12.678
you know, and so it becomes
a little bit expensive

00:24:12.678 --> 00:24:16.170
to capture rats.

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:20.490
So we had a really smart Yale
graduate student, PhD student,

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:21.727
Kate Hacker that said,

00:24:21.727 --> 00:24:23.940
"Hey, maybe there's a
better way to do this."

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:26.280
And this is actually science in action.

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:28.200
And she said, "Okay, what's a cheap way

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:31.950
that we could do is," so
she took ceramic tiles,

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:34.350
each ones cost about 50 cents,

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:36.303
and she painted them with ink.

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:40.260
And then she laid them out like here

00:24:40.260 --> 00:24:41.910
and throughout the community,

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:45.690
and then she started counting
how many paw prints are rats

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:49.343
or sometimes rats like to
scratch at these tiles,

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:56.810
so looking at the tail marks
or the scratch marks of these.

00:24:57.900 --> 00:25:01.770
And using that and by counting
how many are in a tile,

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:04.950
she came up with a way to make a map,

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:06.930
a map of what we call rattiness,

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:10.530
places that are more
rattier than others, okay?

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:12.120
And why is that important?

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:14.820
So remember what an epidemiologist does.

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:16.687
We're not there just to kinda say,

00:25:16.687 --> 00:25:17.967
"Okay, this is where rats live

00:25:17.967 --> 00:25:20.160
and this is where not rats live."

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:24.423
But we wanna use these
maps and in the past,

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:27.240
we used to have to draw all this

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:30.600
but now we have computers that
can really make nice images.

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:33.270
But we wanna draw these maps

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:37.770
to tell us where do we need
to go to do prevention.

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:40.680
Where do we need to know to control rats?

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:42.780
If we can't do it everywhere,

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:46.170
what are the places that
we need to go first, okay?

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:47.940
And that's what this rat tells us.

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:52.560
This tells us a big problem
that I can be standing here

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:54.090
and there'll be a lot of rats,

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:57.363
but if I go 20 meters or 20 yards there,

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:02.070
the concentration of rats
are very different, okay?

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:07.020
And so we have to have not only
people who know where to go,

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:10.290
we also have to have teams that know how

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:11.910
to control these rats.

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:16.140
We either give them
pesticides or we do education

00:26:16.140 --> 00:26:21.140
and we use our youth groups to
actually teach the residents,

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:24.630
many times the adults in the community how

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:29.630
to control their rat populations
through sequestering food,

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:32.790
not allowing refuse to be stored,

00:26:32.790 --> 00:26:36.000
securing water sources
so the rats can't drink

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:41.000
or putting safe water
supplies in these communities.

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:45.690
The other facet is that

00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:49.980
if the rat is urinating the
bacteria in the environment,

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:52.320
what happens if it rains?

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:56.130
And these pictures here
tell an important story

00:26:56.130 --> 00:27:01.130
in the daily lives of these
residents of urban slums.

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:05.760
So this is a very typical scene
where every time it rains,

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:10.530
the residents have to
go into the open sewer,

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:14.280
dig it out so that they
can create a barrier.

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:16.143
So the next time it rains,

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:21.000
it doesn't overflow into their households.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:24.580
This is a resident showing
how high the flooding goes

00:27:27.868 --> 00:27:29.820
and how high the flooding goes.

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:33.660
And so in these events,

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:36.780
you can see many times they're
going bare feet, right,

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:38.670
without any protection.

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:43.670
So these residents get
exposed to an environment,

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:45.990
the sewage water or the dirt

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:48.360
which is contaminated by the bacteria,

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:51.600
and then the bacteria can
penetrate into the skin

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:52.710
and cause an infection,

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:56.070
and that's why we have
these large outbreaks.

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:58.380
So again, we like making maps

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:01.380
so that we know who's at most at risk

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:03.060
and where do we need to go.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:04.743
And when we made these maps,

00:28:07.611 --> 00:28:10.710
and these was actually with
a geographer and an engineer,

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:14.490
you know, team coming in,
helping us to make these maps,

00:28:14.490 --> 00:28:17.511
the people with the highest
risk, yeah go ahead.

00:28:17.511 --> 00:28:18.450
- [Audience Member 2] So like,

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:21.660
you know how the water gets contaminated?

00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:23.430
Wouldn't the plants also be affected?

00:28:23.430 --> 00:28:26.340
So if you eat a plant that
absorbs the (indistinct)?

00:28:26.340 --> 00:28:27.173
- [Albert] Yeah.

00:28:29.318 --> 00:28:30.477
Yeah, that's a good question

00:28:30.477 --> 00:28:34.470
and that also is a way
that people get infected.

00:28:34.470 --> 00:28:37.020
So you could be working there,

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:41.430
you know, and your skin is
exposed, but also if your plants

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:44.340
or many times people will
store food like, you know,

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:47.400
soda or packages, when it floods,

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:50.343
that all will get contaminated
and when they eat it,

00:28:51.719 --> 00:28:54.387
the bacteria can enter into the mouth,

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:59.640
what we call the oral cavity
and cause an infection.

00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:00.870
So that's an important thing.

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:04.230
So you can imagine in this
setting where it rains

00:29:04.230 --> 00:29:07.710
and it gets flooded,
everything gets contaminated.

00:29:07.710 --> 00:29:10.800
And the people who have most
risk are those people who,

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:13.500
not only live by an open sewer,

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:16.770
but those people who are at
the bottom of the valley.

00:29:16.770 --> 00:29:19.863
So just to remind you,
this is an overly poor,

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:23.730
you know, community.

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:26.790
Half of these people
live like $2.50 in a day,

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:30.900
but you can appreciate that
even within those valleys,

00:29:30.900 --> 00:29:33.810
there are people who are
more vulnerable than others.

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:37.890
You can see that these houses
have much better structure

00:29:37.890 --> 00:29:39.870
than the ones at the bottom of the valley

00:29:39.870 --> 00:29:41.610
that get continually flooded.

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:43.260
Many times in the ones at the bottom,

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:45.540
they have to continually raise their house

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:49.590
because all the silt,
all the sediment and dirt

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.393
from the top of their valley floods,

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:55.110
what do you call it, goes down
to the bottom of the valley.

00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:59.010
In this situation, you know,
and many of you come from many,

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:00.450
you know, not only here in New Haven,

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:02.370
but many different parts of the world,

00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:05.790
you'll see this situation
where the most vulnerable,

00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:09.390
those at most risk for disease
are the people who are living

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:11.940
in the worst houses, who
are living by sewers,

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:14.580
who are living in places that get flooded,

00:30:14.580 --> 00:30:16.980
whether it's in Mumbai in India,

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:21.390
or whether it's in Guayaquil in Ecuador,

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:24.240
whether it's in Jakarta in Indonesia,

00:30:24.240 --> 00:30:26.070
any place in the world.

00:30:26.070 --> 00:30:29.250
And by doing this study, and again,

00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:32.550
this was driven by people like you,

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:35.220
young students going out there mapping,

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:38.370
taking a map where now
we have them on tablets,

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:40.410
we give them tablets and drawing out

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:42.630
where all those open sewers are.

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:47.280
And what we are able to
do is to do interventions,

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:50.370
going back to what the
principle of epidemiology,

00:30:50.370 --> 00:30:52.260
our goal is to control disease.

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:57.260
And so we had some of our
students develop rapid tests,

00:30:57.480 --> 00:31:00.360
much like what you use for COVID, okay?

00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:04.680
They identified pieces of the
bacteria that could be used

00:31:04.680 --> 00:31:08.490
to detect the people get infected so

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:11.880
that every time it rains,
we can alert the physician

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:13.920
and give them antibiotics to prevent them

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:17.310
from getting that massive
bleeding in their lungs

00:31:17.310 --> 00:31:18.900
or their renal failure.

00:31:18.900 --> 00:31:20.520
We also did rat control.

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:23.830
We would send out teams
working with the government

00:31:25.433 --> 00:31:28.050
to go target those areas

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:30.150
where the highest rat populations are,

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:33.587
but also the highest risk

00:31:33.587 --> 00:31:36.930
for getting this disease leptospirosis.

00:31:36.930 --> 00:31:40.590
Probably the most important
thing that we did was to work

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:43.230
together with the community and actually

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:45.510
in a community participatory model,

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:48.150
much like what brings you here today

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:49.770
where we're working with leaders,

00:31:49.770 --> 00:31:53.430
we're working with the community.

00:31:53.430 --> 00:31:56.490
And by working together,
and at this time of my life,

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:58.890
I was working with the
Brazilian Ministry of Health,

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:03.890
we're able to convince the
ministry of finance to give money

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:06.870
to close the sewers in all those regions

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:11.340
of the city where they had
open sewers and high risk

00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:13.920
of getting this life-threatening disease.

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:15.840
And by doing all of this,

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:18.060
we were able, within 10 years, to get

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:20.430
about a fivefold reduction.

00:32:20.430 --> 00:32:24.600
We decreased the number
of cases of leptospirosis

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:28.118
that would come into our
hospitals every year by 90%,

00:32:28.118 --> 00:32:30.750
no 80 to 90%.

00:32:30.750 --> 00:32:34.920
So this is a good example of
not only science in action,

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:36.030
but also an example

00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:39.420
of how young people can make
a difference through science.

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:43.560
And the third thing is that
how people from different types

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:47.460
of sciences can work all
together for something

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:51.333
that could provide a societal benefit.

00:32:52.770 --> 00:32:54.682
So yeah, please.

00:32:54.682 --> 00:32:57.480
