00:37:17.460 --> 00:37:20.970
but very few situations where people here,

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:24.123
people like you and I are going
to encounter leptospirosis.

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:29.990
But this also kind of
highlights some key themes

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:33.267
about challenges that your, yeah please.

00:37:33.267 --> 00:37:35.880
(audience member speaking indistinctly)

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:39.120
Rabies, so rats don't carry rabies.

00:37:39.120 --> 00:37:41.220
They carry other diseases
that we worry about.

00:37:41.220 --> 00:37:42.930
This is one of them, right?

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:45.480
But they can also carry other diseases.

00:37:45.480 --> 00:37:48.900
Rabies, we're worried a
little bit more about dogs,

00:37:48.900 --> 00:37:52.020
bats, raccoons, other types of animals,

00:37:52.020 --> 00:37:54.540
sometimes rabbits, okay?

00:37:54.540 --> 00:37:55.383
Yep, please.

00:37:57.332 --> 00:38:01.538
(child speaking indistinctly)

00:38:01.538 --> 00:38:02.377
I'm sorry?

00:38:02.377 --> 00:38:06.210
(child speaking indistinctly)

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:10.980
So rats don't have
rabies. They can't carry.

00:38:10.980 --> 00:38:15.980
So some animals can carry a
disease and not carry others.

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:20.820
And that's the question
for a scientist, right?

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:22.320
You have to do science.

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:23.640
Sometimes we don't really know,

00:38:23.640 --> 00:38:26.430
why certain animals carry some disease

00:38:26.430 --> 00:38:28.470
and others don't carry another.

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:29.640
And that's gonna be important,

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:32.790
so I'll come back to your
question in a second, okay?

00:38:32.790 --> 00:38:36.210
And that's important when
we think about these type

00:38:36.210 --> 00:38:39.030
of emerging infectious diseases,

00:38:39.030 --> 00:38:40.440
but let me go through some of these.

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:45.150
So the big challenges
for my generation was how

00:38:45.150 --> 00:38:48.960
to improve the health of
all, not just the rich

00:38:48.960 --> 00:38:50.343
but also the poor.

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:53.340
Challenge for your generation,

00:38:53.340 --> 00:38:55.320
at least for infectious diseases,

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:56.790
it's gonna be the bigger problems

00:38:56.790 --> 00:38:58.770
and those problems that
are gonna be caused

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:01.050
by rapid urbanization,

00:39:01.050 --> 00:39:04.500
like I showed in all the
communities that we talked to,

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:07.090
all those disorganized communities

00:39:08.945 --> 00:39:10.320
that are rapidly expanding.

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:12.060
The other's climate change.

00:39:12.060 --> 00:39:15.120
You can imagine how, yep, go ahead.

00:39:15.120 --> 00:39:18.450
- [Child 3] Also, COVID actually
started because of that.

00:39:18.450 --> 00:39:19.710
- [Albert] Yeah, exactly.

00:39:19.710 --> 00:39:21.780
Wow this group, Kurt,

00:39:21.780 --> 00:39:24.210
I don't think I have to give this talk.

00:39:24.210 --> 00:39:25.660
I think they know everything.

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:28.113
All right, good, good.

00:39:29.923 --> 00:39:32.340
Yeah, so let me give a good example.

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:34.470
Climate change is gonna be important.

00:39:34.470 --> 00:39:38.670
So remember those people
digging out their sewers?

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:42.210
Well, those people,

00:39:42.210 --> 00:39:46.230
if it rains more and it
rains more violently,

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:48.090
they're gonna have to be exposed more

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:50.883
and more to this disease, okay?

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:54.720
So climate change with these
extreme weather events,

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:57.990
hurricanes, typhoons, cyclones, monsoons,

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:00.000
this is something that's
going to be a challenge

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.080
for all of you in the next decades.

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:07.260
The other is the changing
land use and ecosystems.

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:10.470
You saw the precarious
way these communities,

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:13.080
one billion of our
world's population live.

00:40:13.080 --> 00:40:16.710
And I think it sounds like everyone knows

00:40:16.710 --> 00:40:21.710
that we can get new
infections from animals, okay?

00:40:21.780 --> 00:40:23.340
And there's several good examples of that.

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:25.320
We'll go through it. COVID is one.

00:40:25.320 --> 00:40:27.870
And then the other is
that once we're infected

00:40:27.870 --> 00:40:30.570
with a new infection, we
can spread it quickly.

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:32.913
Why? Because we move much more.

00:40:34.230 --> 00:40:35.760
I think when I was growing up,

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:37.170
for the first 15 years of my life,

00:40:37.170 --> 00:40:40.200
I didn't step outside of the
state of New Jersey, okay?

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:43.470
Now, my children have been
all over the world, right,

00:40:43.470 --> 00:40:45.270
like many of you will, okay?

00:40:45.270 --> 00:40:48.750
So we're moving a lot more
than we did in the past.

00:40:48.750 --> 00:40:51.000
And the other is that our population,

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:52.620
not just here in the United States,

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:55.980
but many places in the
world is getting older

00:40:55.980 --> 00:40:59.550
and older people many
times are more susceptible

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:04.110
or have more risk of
getting severe infections.

00:41:04.110 --> 00:41:06.840
So all of these things
are gonna be challenges

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:08.850
that you're gonna have to deal with,

00:41:08.850 --> 00:41:10.620
you know, in the future.

00:41:10.620 --> 00:41:15.620
And let's get back to the
question before about why animals,

00:41:15.960 --> 00:41:18.720
so you know, some of these
animals carry rabies.

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:22.350
Bats can cause rabies.

00:41:22.350 --> 00:41:25.050
Camels have been reported,
but very infrequently

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:26.970
to cause rabies.

00:41:26.970 --> 00:41:30.750
But there are certain diseases
that we really worry about

00:41:30.750 --> 00:41:34.950
that occur in animals
that may spread to humans.

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:37.203
The big one is influenza.

00:41:38.430 --> 00:41:42.150
There's a current thought
that the 1918 pandemic

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:46.920
that happened was actually
due to across a spillover

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:51.900
or spread of a bird influenza to humans.

00:41:51.900 --> 00:41:54.480
So that's one of the
things that we worry about.

00:41:54.480 --> 00:41:59.160
As we all know, SARS-CoV-1
is another one, okay?

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:02.400
The first SARS virus,
this is back in 2003,

00:42:02.400 --> 00:42:06.510
this was due to spread from a civet

00:42:06.510 --> 00:42:09.810
that was in a marketplace
and went to a restaurant.

00:42:09.810 --> 00:42:12.720
Actually my friend did this investigation

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:16.200
in the people's Republic of
China and found the restaurant

00:42:16.200 --> 00:42:21.167
that actually caused
that spread to humans.

00:42:22.530 --> 00:42:24.510
And there's another virus called MERS,

00:42:24.510 --> 00:42:28.080
which is a cousin of the COVID virus

00:42:28.080 --> 00:42:30.720
which is carried by camels.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:33.330
And bats can do many of these.

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:36.360
So you can see that
there are certain animals

00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:38.280
that can cause infection,

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:41.250
but also certain practices
and certain behaviors

00:42:41.250 --> 00:42:42.840
that place us at risk.

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:45.693
So for example, Ebola, it's bushmeat.

00:42:46.980 --> 00:42:48.960
For SARS-CoV-1,

00:42:48.960 --> 00:42:51.960
it's many times these
what we call wet markets,

00:42:51.960 --> 00:42:55.680
so these markets where
you bring wildlife animal

00:42:55.680 --> 00:42:59.880
that are used for consumption,
or bird influenza.

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:02.460
We have huge farms in the United States

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:07.350
where we produce a lot of
chicken that get infected

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:08.850
with bird influenza.

00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:11.010
These are all potential causes.

00:43:11.010 --> 00:43:12.210
And I think just for the time,

00:43:12.210 --> 00:43:15.210
I'm gonna skip this part and let's end up

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:18.267
with a good example, okay?

00:43:18.267 --> 00:43:20.730
And all of you have passed through this

00:43:20.730 --> 00:43:23.430
and you may pass through
this again in the future,

00:43:23.430 --> 00:43:25.710
but we are leaving the pandemic

00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:28.260
but I think it's good to reflect for you

00:43:28.260 --> 00:43:32.940
about why science is so
important and what it has done

00:43:32.940 --> 00:43:35.160
for us in the last three years.

00:43:35.160 --> 00:43:40.160
So, we really don't know
exactly how the virus spread

00:43:42.780 --> 00:43:45.570
from animals to humans, okay?

00:43:45.570 --> 00:43:47.160
There's still quite a bit of debate,

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:51.180
but we know that that first
initial outbreak happened

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:53.820
in China, caused a large outbreak.

00:43:53.820 --> 00:43:56.880
And what's implicated were
these markets where many times,

00:43:56.880 --> 00:44:01.530
you'll put one animal with
one species on top of another

00:44:01.530 --> 00:44:04.830
and then they're all
breathing or urinating,

00:44:04.830 --> 00:44:06.810
defecating on top of each other

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:11.700
and they're spreading, spreading viruses.

00:44:11.700 --> 00:44:15.093
So that's where that
initial event happened.

00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:18.780
But going back to kinda those drivers

00:44:18.780 --> 00:44:23.780
and why we really worry about
pandemics is this situation,

00:44:23.850 --> 00:44:26.080
these are all the airplane flights

00:44:27.060 --> 00:44:30.000
between all the cities in the world.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.820
So you could take that
map of airplane flights

00:44:32.820 --> 00:44:37.230
and just put that on top of what we saw.

00:44:37.230 --> 00:44:42.030
This is based on using
molecular biology or genomics,

00:44:42.030 --> 00:44:46.800
what we saw about the spread
of the SARS-CoV-2 virus

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:50.730
or the COVID virus in the
first three or four months

00:44:50.730 --> 00:44:52.353
of the pandemic, right?

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:57.720
It's interesting that one
of the only nonstop flights

00:44:57.720 --> 00:45:00.900
from Wuhan, which started,
went exactly to Milan

00:45:00.900 --> 00:45:03.210
because of the fashion industry, you know?

00:45:03.210 --> 00:45:05.490
Wuhan produces a lot of the clothes

00:45:05.490 --> 00:45:07.980
that Italian fashion makers use,

00:45:07.980 --> 00:45:10.590
and there's a direct
flight and Italy was one

00:45:10.590 --> 00:45:13.920
of the first and hardest hit countries

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:18.920
that was affected by the COVID pandemic.

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:22.740
So this is a good example
of how the world is moving

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:27.740
and how we're at increased
risk for once a virus happens,

00:45:28.230 --> 00:45:30.120
that it spreads quickly.

00:45:30.120 --> 00:45:31.560
And these are some of the challenges

00:45:31.560 --> 00:45:35.610
that we faced during the pandemic,

00:45:35.610 --> 00:45:38.160
well first that this was a new virus

00:45:38.160 --> 00:45:40.320
that was never experienced by any of us,

00:45:40.320 --> 00:45:42.570
not only in this room but in the world,

00:45:42.570 --> 00:45:45.810
you know, prior to the pandemic.

00:45:45.810 --> 00:45:49.770
And that virus, because
we never experienced it,

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:51.090
none of us had immunity.

00:45:51.090 --> 00:45:54.633
We're all susceptible to that virus.

00:45:55.740 --> 00:45:59.010
The second is, is that this
was a virus because it was new,

00:45:59.010 --> 00:46:01.140
we didn't understand its biology.

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:03.120
This is the importance of science.

00:46:03.120 --> 00:46:05.454
Without understanding its biology,

00:46:05.454 --> 00:46:09.390
we can't make diagnostic tests,

00:46:09.390 --> 00:46:11.760
we can't make therapies or treatments

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:13.530
to treat people who got sick.

00:46:13.530 --> 00:46:18.530
We can't prevent it with
making vaccines and so forth.

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:22.200
So, that was a key challenge.

00:46:22.200 --> 00:46:26.100
The next was that this was
a highly transmissible virus

00:46:26.100 --> 00:46:28.130
'cause many of you, probably more than 50%

00:46:28.130 --> 00:46:31.500
of you have gotten COVID in this room,

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:33.450
whether you knew it or not.

00:46:33.450 --> 00:46:36.450
But this is a virus that
could be transmitted

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:41.400
before you got sick, which is
important because if I'm sick,

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:42.600
I'll stay in my room

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:46.080
but if I don't know if I can
transmit it before I get sick,

00:46:46.080 --> 00:46:49.140
maybe I don't know and
I'm going to classes

00:46:49.140 --> 00:46:51.180
and meeting people.

00:46:51.180 --> 00:46:53.530
And the other was what
we call super spreading,

00:46:54.630 --> 00:46:59.630
you know, where one person may spread it

00:47:00.390 --> 00:47:02.610
to more people than another person,

00:47:02.610 --> 00:47:04.740
which makes it very difficult to identify,

00:47:04.740 --> 00:47:09.330
who is the person who's
actually causing transmission

00:47:09.330 --> 00:47:10.533
in the community.

00:47:11.580 --> 00:47:13.380
The other problem, as we, you know,

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:16.080
sorely learned in the
beginning of the pandemic is

00:47:16.080 --> 00:47:19.950
that this was a more virulent virus,

00:47:19.950 --> 00:47:24.060
more virulent than influenza,
okay, with the people.

00:47:24.060 --> 00:47:25.950
And then the further challenges,

00:47:25.950 --> 00:47:28.560
as we progressed through the pandemic,

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:31.050
one was mutations, okay?

00:47:31.050 --> 00:47:34.110
Does everybody, have you
heard about mutations?

00:47:34.110 --> 00:47:35.340
All right, good, good.

00:47:35.340 --> 00:47:37.200
So this virus, like all of us,

00:47:37.200 --> 00:47:39.960
we have mutations that are
happening in our bodies

00:47:39.960 --> 00:47:44.960
but this virus had mutations
whose strains were selected

00:47:45.210 --> 00:47:48.540
for, which created more transmissible,

00:47:48.540 --> 00:47:51.030
even more highly transmissible variants,

00:47:51.030 --> 00:47:52.800
innervated the immune response.

00:47:52.800 --> 00:47:55.110
Some of you may have heard
of the Omicron variant.

00:47:55.110 --> 00:47:57.210
That's a good example.

00:47:57.210 --> 00:47:58.620
So this pandemic,

00:47:58.620 --> 00:48:01.710
the pandemic we started
with is not the pandemic

00:48:01.710 --> 00:48:03.420
that we ended with.

00:48:03.420 --> 00:48:04.740
And then I think the last one,

00:48:04.740 --> 00:48:06.270
which was really important is

00:48:06.270 --> 00:48:07.830
that we did a lot of good science.

00:48:07.830 --> 00:48:11.190
We did a lot of catch up,
especially our biologists,

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:13.470
our immunologists, you know, the chemists.

00:48:13.470 --> 00:48:14.303
Yep.

00:48:15.726 --> 00:48:17.670
(Speaker speaking indistinctly)

00:48:17.670 --> 00:48:19.530
Or we can spread to animals.

00:48:19.530 --> 00:48:21.690
So this is a good example.

00:48:21.690 --> 00:48:23.160
Boy, that's a very good question.

00:48:23.160 --> 00:48:25.953
You guys are thinking
about contagion a lot.

00:48:27.190 --> 00:48:32.190
So we can get infections from animals,

00:48:32.460 --> 00:48:36.510
but we can also pass those
infections back to animals.

00:48:36.510 --> 00:48:39.600
So a lot of good examples
of dogs getting COVID,

00:48:39.600 --> 00:48:42.990
cats getting COVID, tigers
in zoos getting COVID,

00:48:42.990 --> 00:48:46.620
giraffes getting COVID and dying of COVID

00:48:46.620 --> 00:48:48.570
that they didn't get from another animal.

00:48:48.570 --> 00:48:50.670
They got it from the zookeeper,

00:48:50.670 --> 00:48:54.180
or they got it from the
pet owner that got COVID,

00:48:54.180 --> 00:48:57.870
and that makes it very
difficult to control, right?

00:48:57.870 --> 00:48:59.100
If it's only humans, well,

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:01.440
there are things we can do
with humans like vaccinate.

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:04.680
But if it's animals that
are infected as well,

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:07.953
those type of diseases
are very difficult to,

00:49:08.850 --> 00:49:11.310
you know, certainly
eliminate or eradicate,

00:49:11.310 --> 00:49:14.193
but also to control in the short term.

00:49:15.240 --> 00:49:17.970
So the other problem
is, is that we did a lot

00:49:17.970 --> 00:49:22.470
of good science in biology
and in the chemistry

00:49:22.470 --> 00:49:24.570
and in the, what do you call it,

00:49:24.570 --> 00:49:27.300
in the immunology of the disease

00:49:27.300 --> 00:49:29.220
and the genetics of the disease.

00:49:29.220 --> 00:49:31.530
But we didn't really do
a lot of good scientists

00:49:31.530 --> 00:49:34.590
and we didn't have a lot of
trained scientists working

00:49:34.590 --> 00:49:38.520
on those type of behavioral interventions,

00:49:38.520 --> 00:49:40.110
those things that we can do

00:49:40.110 --> 00:49:42.390
to make people get vaccinated more

00:49:42.390 --> 00:49:44.820
or make people wear face masks

00:49:44.820 --> 00:49:49.820
or promote social distancing, there.

00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:54.360
So we need more scientists
specifically in this area

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:57.090
that can some give good advice to leaders

00:49:57.090 --> 00:49:59.553
and have the leaders
make the right decisions.

00:50:00.540 --> 00:50:02.730
And this is just the aftermath, right?

00:50:02.730 --> 00:50:05.940
We're kind of in the
aftermath of the pandemic.

00:50:05.940 --> 00:50:08.940
We've had just the United States alone,

00:50:08.940 --> 00:50:12.090
over 1.1 million people who
died during this epidemic.

00:50:12.090 --> 00:50:16.110
So large, devastating
cost, happened over waves.

00:50:16.110 --> 00:50:18.990
Many of our population
have gotten infected.

00:50:18.990 --> 00:50:22.260
Many of our population have
also gotten vaccinated.

00:50:22.260 --> 00:50:26.040
And there are really
good, what do you call it,

00:50:26.040 --> 00:50:27.840
key scientific achievements

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:31.530
and this is really how science
can make for a better society

00:50:31.530 --> 00:50:33.180
and a healthier society.

00:50:33.180 --> 00:50:36.270
We had molecular tests and rapid tests.

00:50:36.270 --> 00:50:38.680
We were able to test the sewage water

00:50:40.307 --> 00:50:43.323
to understand how much transmission
comes in the community.

00:50:44.340 --> 00:50:47.310
We learned early on that social distancing

00:50:47.310 --> 00:50:48.810
and wearing face masks,

00:50:48.810 --> 00:50:53.793
those kind of barrier types of
interventions were effective.

00:50:55.290 --> 00:50:57.630
Within the first six
months of the pandemic,

00:50:57.630 --> 00:51:02.460
we learned that certain
drugs like steroids followed

00:51:02.460 --> 00:51:04.020
by monoclonal antibodies

00:51:04.020 --> 00:51:07.230
and antiviral agents were effective.

00:51:07.230 --> 00:51:10.050
And then I think probably the
most important one was safe

00:51:10.050 --> 00:51:14.850
and effective vaccines,
such as maybe the vaccines

00:51:14.850 --> 00:51:16.950
that many of you in this room have taken,

00:51:16.950 --> 00:51:19.920
these mRNA vaccines.

00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:23.760
So those were really great
breakthroughs that kept,

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:27.420
you know, what could have
been an even more devastating,

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:30.900
pandemic, less so.

00:51:30.900 --> 00:51:32.790
And just to tell you that, you know,

00:51:32.790 --> 00:51:35.970
there are a lot of people
who worked on this at Yale,

00:51:35.970 --> 00:51:37.830
really heroic efforts.

00:51:37.830 --> 00:51:42.060
This is, one group had
the privilege to lead.

00:51:42.060 --> 00:51:45.960
It was called the Yale
Impact team, you know,

00:51:45.960 --> 00:51:47.760
which mounted a response the first week.

00:51:47.760 --> 00:51:52.140
This is March 2nd of 2020, even
before we had the first case

00:51:52.140 --> 00:51:54.780
and doing things like using saliva,

00:51:54.780 --> 00:51:58.980
your spit to detect whether
you're infected or not, okay?

00:51:58.980 --> 00:52:01.710
The other was that wastewater
surveillance setting up

00:52:01.710 --> 00:52:04.500
in New Haven, understanding
whether we had transmission

00:52:04.500 --> 00:52:07.293
or not by testing the sewage water.

00:52:08.520 --> 00:52:11.850
And one of the things that
we had to do was protect,

00:52:11.850 --> 00:52:13.710
like in Brazil, those photos,

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:17.490
the people who are most
vulnerable in our population.

00:52:17.490 --> 00:52:18.780
And who were they?

00:52:18.780 --> 00:52:20.220
They were, first of all,

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:22.710
the people living in the
cities, Hartford, Waterbury,

00:52:22.710 --> 00:52:24.630
New Haven, Bridgeport, Stanford.

00:52:24.630 --> 00:52:27.810
Those cities, which were
also the most vulnerable,

00:52:27.810 --> 00:52:31.410
had the highest what we call
social vulnerability index.

00:52:31.410 --> 00:52:34.170
These were the cities that got hit hardest

00:52:34.170 --> 00:52:37.200
in our communities during
that terrible first wave.

00:52:37.200 --> 00:52:39.960
The other population
was their nursing homes.

00:52:39.960 --> 00:52:44.960
11% of all our nursing
home residents, you know,

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:50.000
died during that first one
month of that pandemic.

00:52:50.580 --> 00:52:54.120
And that's when, you know,
this is I think the other part

00:52:54.120 --> 00:52:57.060
which is that there's one
thing that we do day-to-day

00:52:57.060 --> 00:52:58.950
whether it's in the
laboratory or in the field,

00:52:58.950 --> 00:53:03.950
or in the community, to do
science and to get evidence,

00:53:04.920 --> 00:53:09.270
but the other key part is
to work with the leaders

00:53:09.270 --> 00:53:13.350
in making, translating that
evidence and that science

00:53:13.350 --> 00:53:15.540
and putting it into action.

00:53:15.540 --> 00:53:16.373
And, you know,

00:53:16.373 --> 00:53:19.590
I had the privilege of being
called on, as Kurt said,

00:53:19.590 --> 00:53:22.800
by Governor Lamont to
co-chair with Indra Nooyi,

00:53:22.800 --> 00:53:24.930
who was the former CEO of PepsiCo

00:53:24.930 --> 00:53:27.120
so somebody who wasn't a scientist,

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:30.510
but was important to get a balance so

00:53:30.510 --> 00:53:35.510
that we could think of how we
could first protect the state

00:53:35.670 --> 00:53:37.277
and then how we could reopen it

00:53:37.277 --> 00:53:40.290
'cause many of people had
lost their employment,

00:53:40.290 --> 00:53:43.410
people they needed who were
working in the restaurants,

00:53:43.410 --> 00:53:46.620
who are working as taxi
drivers and so forth,

00:53:46.620 --> 00:53:48.750
lost their jobs during that period.

00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:50.910
So how could we safely reopen the state?

00:53:50.910 --> 00:53:53.070
And I think the major guiding principle,

00:53:53.070 --> 00:53:55.830
and this is I think really as hopefully,

00:53:55.830 --> 00:54:00.480
you know, as a justification
why science is important is

00:54:00.480 --> 00:54:04.620
that you need science in
order to do everything else,

00:54:04.620 --> 00:54:08.820
to reopen the economy, to
keep people, hospitals safe,

00:54:08.820 --> 00:54:13.740
to keep people protected, to
minimize harm that may occur.

00:54:13.740 --> 00:54:18.740
If we were too rigorous in
keeping everybody at home,

00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:22.350
you know, safe at home, people
would be losing their jobs.

00:54:22.350 --> 00:54:25.530
People would be losing their income.

00:54:25.530 --> 00:54:29.100
They wouldn't have money
to feed their children.

00:54:29.100 --> 00:54:32.010
So one of the two things,
just wanted to highlight

00:54:32.010 --> 00:54:36.570
of that plan was, we were
called in on April 13th

00:54:36.570 --> 00:54:38.580
which was the peak of the pandemic,

00:54:38.580 --> 00:54:41.220
first wave in Connecticut.

00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:42.053
As I mentioned,

00:54:42.053 --> 00:54:45.450
11% by then of our nursing
home residents had died.

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:47.250
And one of the most important things is

00:54:47.250 --> 00:54:49.230
that we didn't have enough tests.

00:54:49.230 --> 00:54:53.880
We were doing 200 tests per week.

00:54:53.880 --> 00:54:56.610
We were having all of our
laboratories in the state,

00:54:56.610 --> 00:54:59.250
Jackson Labs, Yale, Hartford Healthcare,

00:54:59.250 --> 00:55:01.950
all of them were developing new tests

00:55:01.950 --> 00:55:04.290
but we had to get them to the people

00:55:04.290 --> 00:55:07.350
because the hospitals wouldn't
allow anybody to come in

00:55:07.350 --> 00:55:09.930
unless you were diagnosed with COVID.

00:55:09.930 --> 00:55:14.670
So we went within two months
from about 200 kits per day

00:55:14.670 --> 00:55:19.080
to about 20,000 kits today by
using all the laboratories,

00:55:19.080 --> 00:55:21.600
all the scientists, just
calling them all up.

00:55:21.600 --> 00:55:23.700
It was sort of like
World War II, you know,

00:55:23.700 --> 00:55:25.560
calling everybody up who could do a test

00:55:25.560 --> 00:55:28.320
and getting them on board.

00:55:28.320 --> 00:55:31.140
The second thing that we
did was to vaccinate people.

00:55:31.140 --> 00:55:36.140
Once the vaccines were approved
on December 22nd, 2020,

00:55:36.420 --> 00:55:37.950
rolled them out.

00:55:37.950 --> 00:55:40.110
Many of you may have remembered,

00:55:40.110 --> 00:55:42.990
we had these large testing centers as well

00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:44.400
as large vaccination centers.

00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:45.930
You'd go into your car,

00:55:45.930 --> 00:55:50.430
roll up your sleeve, open the
window and you get vaccinated.

00:55:50.430 --> 00:55:52.620
So all of that was driven by science,

00:55:52.620 --> 00:55:56.340
driven by best practices
that we had at that time

00:55:56.340 --> 00:55:59.790
and you could see that
horrible first epidemic

00:55:59.790 --> 00:56:03.210
and then how that decreased, you know,

00:56:03.210 --> 00:56:05.850
through time pretty much
because of vaccination

00:56:05.850 --> 00:56:07.950
and access to healthcare.

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:12.180
And Kurt, I'm going over a little bit.

00:56:12.180 --> 00:56:15.960
Is that okay, just two more minutes?

00:56:15.960 --> 00:56:20.250
Yeah, so I think the
most important thing is

00:56:20.250 --> 00:56:22.680
that who you are, and this goes back

00:56:22.680 --> 00:56:25.380
to the foundational
principles of epidemiology,

00:56:25.380 --> 00:56:29.160
our goal is to control disease
and protect the population.

00:56:29.160 --> 00:56:31.770
And the populations that suffered most

00:56:31.770 --> 00:56:33.330
during that initial phase

00:56:33.330 --> 00:56:36.810
of the epidemic were the
nursing home population,

00:56:36.810 --> 00:56:40.650
but also our African-American
and Latino populations.

00:56:40.650 --> 00:56:45.090
African-Americans and
Latinos died five times more

00:56:45.090 --> 00:56:46.740
than our white population in our state

00:56:46.740 --> 00:56:48.960
during that first wave.

00:56:48.960 --> 00:56:52.920
And the only answer to
that was science, okay,

00:56:52.920 --> 00:56:56.517
getting testing and vaccination
to those populations.

00:56:56.517 --> 00:57:01.517
And you can see by the end,
by our third pandemic wave,

00:57:02.100 --> 00:57:04.650
we had no difference between mortality

00:57:04.650 --> 00:57:06.930
between our African American population

00:57:06.930 --> 00:57:08.520
and white population.

00:57:08.520 --> 00:57:12.150
And still an elevated population,

00:57:12.150 --> 00:57:15.690
increased risk of dying
among our Latino population.

00:57:15.690 --> 00:57:18.480
So more work needs to be done,

00:57:18.480 --> 00:57:22.230
but large drops in mortality that occurred

00:57:22.230 --> 00:57:25.800
because of the public health
action driven by science.

00:57:25.800 --> 00:57:28.510
So let me end up with just two slides

00:57:29.910 --> 00:57:31.470
and again, thank you Kurt,

00:57:31.470 --> 00:57:35.040
It's really an honor and
a privilege to be talking,

00:57:35.040 --> 00:57:38.700
especially this is the first
talk after the pandemic.

00:57:38.700 --> 00:57:41.130
But let me go back a
little bit and just think

00:57:41.130 --> 00:57:44.400
about what helped me when
I was in your position.

00:57:44.400 --> 00:57:46.470
I had really great mentors.

00:57:46.470 --> 00:57:49.770
My high school chemistry
teacher was the one, my mother

00:57:49.770 --> 00:57:52.140
and my high school chemistry
teacher were probably,

00:57:52.140 --> 00:57:54.180
the two most important people in my lives

00:57:54.180 --> 00:57:58.590
in teaching me about
science and motivating me

00:57:58.590 --> 00:58:00.360
and enjoying science, right,

00:58:00.360 --> 00:58:02.283
and enjoying to talk about science.

00:58:03.270 --> 00:58:06.150
I think the other people in my
lives were kind of the people

00:58:06.150 --> 00:58:09.520
during my college, you know, professors

00:58:10.530 --> 00:58:12.397
in medical school where they said,

00:58:12.397 --> 00:58:13.620
"Why don't you do this?

00:58:13.620 --> 00:58:16.740
You know, pick an
important question, okay?

00:58:16.740 --> 00:58:18.120
And then go for it."

00:58:18.120 --> 00:58:20.850
And for all of you in this room,

00:58:20.850 --> 00:58:22.170
particularly the young generation,

00:58:22.170 --> 00:58:24.360
these are the important questions, right?

00:58:24.360 --> 00:58:28.140
How do we reduce inequalities?
How do we reduce poverty?

00:58:28.140 --> 00:58:31.140
How do we do we mitigate hunger?

00:58:31.140 --> 00:58:34.290
How do we address climate change?

00:58:34.290 --> 00:58:37.620
How do we do affordable and clean energy?

00:58:37.620 --> 00:58:38.910
Those are the key issues.

00:58:38.910 --> 00:58:40.560
This is not just for our public health,

00:58:40.560 --> 00:58:45.560
but this involves physics,
astronomy, it involves, you know,

00:58:45.750 --> 00:58:50.070
sociology, anthropology, all
of the different sciences.

00:58:50.070 --> 00:58:53.370
The second is, is that
learn constantly and put

00:58:53.370 --> 00:58:54.870
in practice the new things you learn.

00:58:54.870 --> 00:58:57.600
I'm still learning today and importantly,

00:58:57.600 --> 00:59:00.210
I'm learning from people
much younger than me.

00:59:00.210 --> 00:59:02.820
I was just in Brazil a month ago.

00:59:02.820 --> 00:59:05.100
I'm learning from the 14 and 15 year olds.

00:59:05.100 --> 00:59:06.690
They're telling me new things

00:59:06.690 --> 00:59:09.140
about something I thought
I knew something about,

00:59:10.380 --> 00:59:12.000
every time I meet them.

00:59:12.000 --> 00:59:15.240
I think the other thing that
we don't really emphasize

00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:18.480
as much, but it's to
talk about science, okay?

00:59:18.480 --> 00:59:20.370
It's fun to talk about science,

00:59:20.370 --> 00:59:23.280
but also to be an advocate for science.

00:59:23.280 --> 00:59:26.100
You are the future leaders
and we will need more

00:59:26.100 --> 00:59:28.510
and more scientists like you

00:59:29.400 --> 00:59:34.400
to advise leaders, such as
the governor or the president

00:59:35.700 --> 00:59:37.920
and so forth about what's the best way

00:59:37.920 --> 00:59:39.390
to move forward, okay?

00:59:39.390 --> 00:59:43.140
So your role in society is enormous.

00:59:43.140 --> 00:59:45.270
So with that, let me end.

00:59:45.270 --> 00:59:47.250
Apologies Kurt for going over,

00:59:47.250 --> 00:59:50.415
but just to say thank you
to everyone, and welcome.

00:59:50.415 --> 00:59:53.650
(audience applauding)

00:59:53.650 --> 00:59:56.317
(mellow jingle)

